            "Tumors cause symptoms in four main ways, often in combination:\n\n- Headache with features of [[raised-intracranial-pressure|raised intracranial pressure]]: worse on waking or lying flat, worse with coughing or straining, with vomiting, and with [[papilledema|papilledema]]. Isolated headache with a normal examination is rarely the only sign of a tumor; the company it keeps matters.\n- Seizures, often [[focal-seizure|focal]], sometimes the first and only symptom. A first seizure in an adult needs brain imaging.\n- Focal deficits that progress over weeks: weakness, language difficulty, visual field loss, or imbalance, depending on where the tumor sits. The tempo (subacute, building) separates this from the sudden onset of a stroke.\n- Personality and cognitive change: apathy, disinhibition, poor planning, or confusion, especially with frontal or corpus callosum tumors. These are easily mistaken for depression or dementia.\n\nTwo routes are common in practice: a patient with a known cancer who develops new neurological symptoms, and a patient with no known cancer whose first sign is neurological.",
