            "CT is often the first scan in the emergency department and shows mass, edema, hemorrhage, and hydrocephalus. The key test is MRI with and without gadolinium, which shows the lesion, its enhancement, edema, and number much better. [[gadolinium-enhancement|Enhancement]] marks breakdown of the blood-brain barrier.\n\nFirst decide whether the lesion is inside the brain (intra-axial) or outside it ([[extra-axial|extra-axial]]). Then look at number, location, and pattern:\n\n- Multiple lesions at the gray-white junction, each with surrounding edema: [[brain-metastasis|metastases]] until proven otherwise.\n- A single irregular [[ring-enhancing-lesion|ring-enhancing]] lesion with a necrotic center and surrounding edema, often crossing the corpus callosum: [[glioblastoma|glioblastoma]] is a leading possibility, along with a solitary metastasis and abscess.\n- A homogeneously (solidly) enhancing lesion next to the ventricles or in the corpus callosum, in an immunocompetent adult: [[primary-cns-lymphoma|primary CNS lymphoma]].\n- An extra-axial, dural-based, homogeneously enhancing lesion, often with a [[dural-tail|dural tail]], that pushes the brain inward rather than growing from it: [[meningioma|meningioma]].\n\nThese patterns raise probabilities; they do not make the diagnosis.",
