          body:
            "A [[ring-enhancing-lesion|ring-enhancing lesion]] has a classic differential:\n\n- [[brain-abscess|Abscess]]: smooth thin rim, with central restricted diffusion on [[diffusion-weighted-imaging|diffusion-weighted imaging]]; look for a source and fever.\n- [[glioblastoma|Glioblastoma]]: thick irregular rim, necrosis, infiltrating edema.\n- [[brain-metastasis|Metastasis]]: often multiple, at the gray-white junction, in a person who may have a known cancer.\n- Tumefactive [[demyelination|demyelination]]: often an incomplete (open) ring with less mass effect than its size suggests.\n- [[toxoplasmosis|Toxoplasmosis]]: multiple lesions, often in the basal ganglia, in advanced HIV or other severe immunosuppression.\n\nThe workup combines the MRI with the clinical context: HIV test, a search for a primary cancer (CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis) when metastasis is possible, blood cultures and a dental and sinus source when abscess is possible. Lumbar puncture is avoided when there is [[mass-effect|mass effect]].\n\nTissue makes the diagnosis: [[stereotactic-biopsy|stereotactic biopsy]] or resection, planned with neurosurgery. If there is a known cancer, an easier biopsy site outside the brain may give the answer.\n\nCorticosteroids reduce tumor edema, but when [[primary-cns-lymphoma|lymphoma]] is possible they should be held until tissue is obtained, unless there is life-threatening mass effect. Lymphoma cells are killed by corticosteroids, so the lesion can shrink or vanish and the biopsy can come back nondiagnostic, delaying treatment.",
